eliberately reduces the rate of change to be comfortable – yet it’s still faster than what our Eustachian tubes can handle for many people. - In an elevator, there’s no pressurization – the elevator car is open to the outside air (via vents), so the pressure changes exactly as the building’s air does. The change is usually gradual enough to go unnoticed. ### 4. Individual variability Some people do feel their ears clog in very tall building elevators, especially if they have colds or allergies. But for most, the combination of a smaller altitude gain and a slower effective rate (because the elevator stops at floors, breaking up the change) makes it less noticeable. ### Summary - **Planes and mountain drives** involve large pressure changes over a short time, easily triggering clogging. - **Elevators** typically involve smaller height gains, and the pressure change is often too small or too slow (even in tall buildings) to cause significant discomfort – though it can happen in the world’s tallest structures. So it’s not that it’s impossible, but the conditions are usually milder in an elevator.
